{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 35, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199. He was a significant figure in the Crusades and a powerful ruler in the Levant. His reign was marked by both military campaigns and diplomatic maneuvering aimed at securing and expanding his territory against Muslim forces.\n\n*Early Life and Accession*\n\nRaymond was born around 1120 the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Ermengarde of Montferrat. He inherited the County of Tripoli upon his father's death in 1154. His early years were dominated by consolidating his authority within the county and dealing with internal challenges. He faced opposition from local barons and had to assert his power to maintain stability. He engaged in military campaigns to suppress rebellions and solidify his control over the region. His marriage to Countess Maria of Antioch strengthened his position and provided him with valuable alliances.\n\nRaymond III faced constant threats from the Zengid dynasty rulers of Syria. He engaged in a series of conflicts with Nur ad-Din and later Saladin. These wars involved sieges battles and shifting alliances. He often sought support from other Crusader states like the Kingdom of Jerusalem to bolster his defenses. His strategic decisions and military leadership were crucial in preserving Tripoli's independence for a time. He invested in strengthening the city's fortifications recognizing its importance as a strategic stronghold.\n\n*Military Campaigns and Conflicts*\n\nRaymond III's reign witnessed numerous military campaigns. He fought against the Zengids in the 1160s and 1170s. These conflicts involved sieges of key cities and battles for control of territory. He also engaged in skirmishes with other Muslim rulers in the region. His military successes helped to maintain Tripoli's autonomy but he never fully eliminated the threat from his enemies. He was known for his skillful defense of Tripoli and his ability to adapt to changing circumstances. He employed a combination of defensive strategies and offensive raids to protect his territory.\n\nRaymond III played a role in the Third Crusade though he did not directly participate in the major battles. He provided logistical support to the Crusader armies and sought to secure alliances with them. He recognized the importance of maintaining good relations with the Crusaders to ensure the survival of his county. He was also involved in diplomatic efforts to negotiate truces and treaties with Muslim rulers.\n\n*Legacy and Death*\n\nRaymond III died in 1199 and was succeeded by his nephew Raymond IV. His reign is remembered as a period of stability and prosperity for the County of Tripoli. He successfully defended Tripoli against numerous threats and maintained its independence for several decades. He was a skilled military leader and a shrewd diplomat. His efforts to strengthen the county's fortifications and maintain alliances with other Crusader states contributed to its survival. He is considered one of the most important rulers in the history of the County of Tripoli. His legacy continued to influence the region for many years after his death. He left behind a county that was relatively well-defended and economically prosperous a testament to his leadership and strategic vision.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0021344278939068317}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.2904873192310333}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.3512556552886963}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.06013408675789833}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.00019393471302464604}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -2.610649426